    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="2" relname="background">This article is an abstract of a collection of articles of the same name.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="35" relname="span">Although this article was penned in 1990,</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="28" relname="span">it introduces some interesting phrases and ideas that are of use even today.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="30" relname="span">The article positions the "interface" as a contact surface that must reflect the properties of the two interactors,</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="4" relname="elaboration">in our case that is the interface between the human and the computer (or application).</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="30" relname="elaboration">It explains that the greater the difference between the two entities, the greater the importance of the interface in lessoning those differences.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="28" relname="elaboration">The article suggests focusing on the user, the user tasks, and user control.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="31" relname="span">It simply states that interface design is hard.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="27" relname="span">The computer analyst alone can no longer do the job.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="9" relname="elaboration">Psychologists, graphic designers, writers, and industrial designers are all needed, each with their own success criteria.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="25" relname="background">The article suggests that the key questions to ask during design are:</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="25" relname="list">What do the users want to do</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="25" relname="list">and How can we think about it so that the interface empowers the user by providing them with ease of use.</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="23" relname="conjunction">It proposes that the interface must be made to blend with the task</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="23" relname="conjunction">and defines intuitiveness as insuring that the interface is simple, adaptive, and self-explanatory.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="34" relname="span">The proposal is that designers should draw on games interfaces for insight.</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="22" relname="span">They argue that games operate in a more demanding market,</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="17" relname="elaboration">where they must be both fun and functional.</segment>
        <group id="19" type="span"/>
